hose allegations were pure  disinformation the Kenyan government  didn't respond to questions about our  story now not all of China's Investments  have been a bust in fact another project  in Kenya this super highway has been  economically viable and has improved  commuting Times by more than an hour  according to researchers at William and  Mary's Aid data research lab but as  those earlier examples show the complex  and nearly impossible to track Belton  Road initiative has run into some major  trouble obviously the fact that the  program now needs to be retooled shows  the limits to XI jinping's Ambitions of  reshaping the global order with Chinese  money and that's why Chinese officials  are pivoting to Belton Road 2.0  something wsj's Chief China  correspondent Ling Ling Wei says is a  priority at the highest levels of the  Chinese government the Chinese now want  to be more prudent and more selective in  terms of who they lend money to so here  are five ways that Belton Road could  change according to researchers who  study the program and people familiar  with policy making in Beijing first a  Slimmer portfolio Chinese banks have  already sharply reduced lending in  low-income countries and for now they've  stopped investing in some countries  altogether like Russia and Egypt that's  according to a report by the green  finance and Development Center at foodon  University in Shanghai secondly Chinese  banks are likely to favor smaller more  Nimble projects like hydropower plants  and solar farms and move away from  financing Mega projects like that  Railway in Kenya third they'll probably  invest more in digital infrastructure  like 5G networks and less in hard  infrastructure like roads and ports  already belt and Road engagement in the  tech sector has increased 300 percent  compared to last year according to that  food on University study fourth the ways  in which China finances projects is also  likely to shift for example instead of  lending directly to Central governments  which report public debt on government  balance sheets Chinese banks are likely  to invest more in public-private  Partnerships and Chinese Banks could  Syndicate more loans which essentially  allows them to share more risk with  other financial institutions these  practices could help China avoid bad  Investments and they could potentially  help root out corruption among the  governments that borrow money from China  but researchers say host governments  could still be on the hook for what they  call Hidden public debt and fifth  experts say that China may need to start  giving more money in the form of Aid and  less money in the form of loans if they  want Belton Road to be sustainable  according to an analysis by a data China  has provided nine dollars of debt for  every one dollar of Aid to low and  middle income countries it's the  opposite for the United States which  provides nine dollars of aid for every  one dollar of debt but there are some  major challenges when it comes to this  belt and Road overhaul in order for  China to move forward experts say it'll  have to renegotiate debt and potentially  even accept some losses when it comes to  the countries that already owe it money  economists estimate that nearly 60  percent of China's overseas loans are  now held by countries considered to be  an economic distress and some of those  countries like Sri Lanka and Zambia are  already experiencing debt crises balance  really are piling up they have to deal  with it if China is too lenient with  those countries it could Stoke tensions  at home where Chinese officials facing a  property crisis have resisted bailing  out the real estate sector but if China  refuses to take a haircut and accept  losses it could fuel anti-china  sentiment abroad and jeopardize  important political relationships plus  China now has competition on the global  stage we think that there's a much more  Equitable way to provide for the needs  of countries around the wo